\todo For the time being, I'm keeping a detailed documentation of how to build an application from scratch in \ref swift_building_phide heading in tree htmlroject_from_scratch


# A first Swift 2 application

Our interface for Swift 2 is almost completely realised in Python.
We configure the whole simulation in Python, but typically make the key
steps of a simulation point to generic C++ routines which are shipped with
Peano 4.
Alterantively, we can inject C++ code manually into the Python code (which is
great for prototyping).
Once the Python script is executed, it generates a plain C++ code with a
makefile which waves Peano's core and the SPH kernels together.

We start with the creation of a Swift 2 project:


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.py}
project = swift2.Project( namespace=['''benchmarks''', '''swift2''', '''planetorbit'''],
                          project_name='''planetorbit''',
                          executable=orbit-exec )
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


Next, we have to add a particle species.
The definition of a particle is straightforward. We employ a tool called
DaStGen 2 which really just is a set of Python classes which represent the
attributes of a C++ class:
We build up a set of Python objects sketching a C++ struct and Swift 2 and
DaStGen eventually dump them into proper C++ code from which it is embedded into the
actual simulator.


~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~{.py}
     import dastgen2

     particle = peano4.toolbox.particles.Particle('''Particle''')

     v_attr = peano4.dastgen2.Peano4DoubleArray('''v''','''Dimensions''')
     mass_attr = dastgen2.attributes.Double('''mass''')

     particle.data.add_attribute(v_attr)
     particle.data.add_attribute(mass_attr)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


Though conceptionally a plain C++ pretty printer, the DaStGen2 Python API
can create some fancy C++ annotations. You can, for example, specify ranges
in which integers live, or you can prescribe the precision you wanna have
for floating point values. If you have a specialised LLVM compiler, these
annotations will be used to generate aggressively optimised code.


*/
